  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1254 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nClouds continue to clear from west to east this afternoon as dry\nair filters in and a cold front will gradually pass through into\ntonight. Strong northwesterly cold air advection will develop\nwith a tightening pressure gradient, leading to blustery\nconditions on Thursday. Temperatures won't get overly chilly\ntonight, but some lows around 40 are possible up around\nKoochiching county. The next cold night will be Thursday night\ninto Friday as a surface high moves in from the west. This won't\nbe a perfect radiational cooling night as we should have some\nwinds still sticking around, but we should still have widespread\ntemperatures in the 33 to 36 degree range and some areas falling\nto or below freezing, especially the usual cold spots and also\nmore widespread in north-central Minnesota (40-60% chance for\ntemperatures falling below freezing there). Widespread frost\nheadlines will likely be needed, as well as some freeze\nheadlines.\n\nOn Friday, look for a seasonably cool and dry day with highs in\nthe 50s/60s. There will be at least localized concern for near\nfreezing and freezing temperatures again Friday night,\nespecially in northwest Wisconsin and the tip of the Arrowhead\nwhere the influence of departing high pressure will still be\nthere. Warm air advection will develop with southerly flow\nstarting Friday night, however, so expect increasing clouds in\nnortheast Minnesota. A clipper is still looking like it will\npass through on Saturday, with an axis of mainly light rain\nlooking like it will target the Canadian border. Overall,\nrainfall amounts still look quite light from this. A few places\ncould sneak up to a quarter of an inch total, but less for most\nplaces that see rain. The rain may be pretty limited in coverage\nto the Iron Range and points north. \n\nAnother cold front will pass through going into Sunday,\nresulting in another seasonably cool day and breezy northwest\nwinds. High pressure moves in again Sunday night, and with this,\nwe'll be looking at one of the coldest nights of the fall season\nso far. Widespread temperatures at or below freezing are likely\n(40-80% chance). Areas around Lake Superior may be spared from \na freeze, but it will probably be only a few miles inland where \nfreezing temperatures are found. This may be a season-ending\nfreeze for many places. Probabilities of temperatures 28 degrees\nor lower are 10-30% at this time, so we will have to wait a few\ndays and see how trends change.\n\nThere will be an upper-level trough just northeast into Ontario,\nproducing a few rain/snow showers, and parts of the UP might see\nsome of their first lake-effect rain/snow showers. We should\nremain dry into Monday with sunshine as high pressure passes\nthrough.\n\nSoutherly flow and an upper-level trough could bring more rain\nchances to the region early next week along with moderating\ntemperatures.",
